Doubly linked lists, for when elements must not move: their addresses stay valid through
insertions, removals, sorting and splicing, and both ends cost O(1).

| Type | Nodes | Fails |
|---|---|---|
| `list<T, A>` | one node per element, from A | on allocation (`append`, `insert`, `clone`) |
| `intrusive_list<T, Tag>` | the links live in the elements | never: it allocates nothing |

Both have `append`, `prepend`, `insert` before a position, `pop`, `pop_front`, `first`, `last`,
`remove`, `reverse`, `splice` (moves all of another list in O(1)), and a stable merge `sort` that
relinks nodes and never moves an element. `list` adds `find`, `count`, `contains`, `retain`,
`extend`, `clone` and `cleanup`.

```c++
#include "ca/array.hpp"
#include "ca/list.hpp"
#include "ca/utility.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::list<int> l{};
	if (!l.extend({5, 3, 9}) || !l.prepend(1) || !l.append(7))
		return 1;
	const int* const three = &*l.find(3).value_or(l.end());
	l.sort();
	if (&*l.find(3).value_or(l.end()) != three)
		return 2;

	auto at = l.find(5);
	if (!at || !l.insert(*at, 4))
		return 3;
	if (!l.retain([](const int& v) noexcept { return v != 9; }))
		return 4;

	ca::list<int> tail{};
	if (!tail.extend({100, 200}))
		return 5;
	l.splice(l.end(), ca::move(tail));
	int expected[] = {1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 100, 200};
	ca::usz i = 0U;
	for (const int v : l)
		if (v != expected[i++])
			return 6;
	return i == 7U && tail.empty() && l.cleanup() ? 0 : 7;
}
```

### Intrusive lists

An element joins an `intrusive_list<T, Tag>` by deriving `ca::list_link<T, Tag>` once per list it
can be in; the Tag tells the links apart, so one object can sit in several lists at once (a task in
a run queue and in a timer list). The list stores nothing per element and allocates nothing, so
no operation can fail. Misuse traps: linking an element that is already linked, or removing one
that is not. Links and lists are trivially destructible, so a trap can unwind past them (see
`ca::context_manager`): an element leaves its list before it is destroyed, and a list is cleared
before it goes away, unchecked.

```c++
#include "ca/intrusive_list.hpp"
#include "ca/list_link.hpp"

struct by_priority;

struct task final : ca::list_link<task>, ca::list_link<task, by_priority> {
	int id;
	int priority;
	task(const int identifier, const int level) noexcept : id{identifier}, priority{level} {
	}
};

auto main(void) -> int {
	task a{1, 3};
	task b{2, 1};
	task c{3, 2};
	ca::intrusive_list<task> arrival;
	ca::intrusive_list<task, by_priority> urgent;
	arrival.append(a);
	arrival.append(b);
	arrival.append(c);
	for (task& t : arrival)
		urgent.append(t);
	urgent.sort([](const task& l, const task& r) noexcept { return l.priority < r.priority; });

	const bool ordered = urgent.first().value_or(a).id == 2 && arrival.first().value_or(c).id == 1;
	arrival.remove(b);
	const bool removed = arrival.size() == 2U && urgent.size() == 3U;
	arrival.clear();
	urgent.clear();
	return ordered && removed ? 0 : 1;
}
```

`list` has no sentinel node, so the list object itself holds no self-pointer and relocates with a
memcpy; `end()` is a null node that remembers its list, so `--end()` still reaches the last
element, and like every iterator it is invalidated when the list object moves.
